    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(5))  # F0..F4\n    customers = list(range(15))  # C0..C14\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(33, 1), (4, 44), (32, 91), (58, 49), (7, 29)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(66, 88), (8, 6), (55, 43), (38, 68), (22, 55), (24, 22), (66, 93), (75, 59), (50, 18), (70, 82), (86, 17), (89, 34), (43, 61), (83, 33), (5, 13)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [316.0, 124.0, 219.0, 164.0, 139.0]\n    customer_demands = [38.0, 44.0, 28.0, 24.0, 60.0, 45.0, 42.0, 11.0, 22.0, 40.0, 19.0, 19.0, 16.0, 37.0, 53.0]\n    fixed_costs = [207.0, 378.0, 161.0, 254.0, 276.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.88  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Decision memo \u2014 Which warehouses to open and how to serve our customers at the lowest cost\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point on our distribution footprint. We have a set of potential warehouse sites and a defined group of customers we need to serve. The question is which warehouses we should open and how to route shipments so that every customer is covered, all facility limits are respected, and our total cost is as low as possible.\n\nWhat we have\n- Candidate facility locations (map coordinates): Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (33, 1)\n  - F1: (4, 44)\n  - F2: (32, 91)\n  - F3: (58, 49)\n  - F4: (7, 29)\n- Customer locations (map coordinates): Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (66, 88)\n  - C1: (8, 6)\n  - C2: (55, 43)\n  - C3: (38, 68)\n  - C4: (22, 55)\n  - C5: (24, 22)\n  - C6: (66, 93)\n  - C7: (75, 59)\n  - C8: (50, 18)\n  - C9: (70, 82)\n  - C10: (86, 17)\n  - C11: (89, 34)\n  - C12: (43, 61)\n  - C13: (83, 33)\n  - C14: (5, 13)\n- Facility handling capacities: [316.0, 124.0, 219.0, 164.0, 139.0]\n- Customer demand forecasts: [38.0, 44.0, 28.0, 24.0, 60.0, 45.0, 42.0, 11.0, 22.0, 40.0, 19.0, 19.0, 16.0, 37.0, 53.0]\n- Fixed cost to open each facility: [207.0, 378.0, 161.0, 254.0, 276.0]\n- Transport rate for shipments: 0.88\n\nWhat this means operationally\n- Opening a warehouse triggers its fixed cost. If a site isn\u2019t opened, it cannot ship.\n- Each warehouse can only handle up to its capacity; we cannot exceed the limits noted above.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied.\n- Shipping costs are driven by how far we move product from an open site to each customer and by how much we ship. Shorter hauls generally reduce transport spend; longer hauls increase it.\n\nThe core trade-off\n- Opening more warehouses can place inventory closer to customers, reducing transportation spend but adding upfront and ongoing fixed facility costs.\n- Opening fewer warehouses cuts fixed costs but may push us into longer delivery distances and higher transportation spend.\n- Capacity matters: even if a site is well-located, it may not be able to handle all the demand in its vicinity, so we need to balance flows across open sites.\n\nDecision we need to make\n- Which subset of the candidate warehouses to open.\n- How to allocate shipments from the open warehouses to customers so that all demand is met without exceeding any facility\u2019s capacity.\n\nBusiness goal\n- Minimize total cost: the sum of facility opening costs and shipping costs. In practice, that means finding the right mix of sites to open and assigning customer orders to those sites in a way that balances fixed costs with distance-driven transport spend.\n\nWhat you\u2019ll receive from this analysis\n- A recommended set of facilities to open and a customer-to-facility shipping plan.\n- A clear breakdown of total expected cost (opening plus transport).\n- Visibility into the cost implications of alternative choices (e.g., opening an additional site vs. consolidating volume at fewer locations).\n\nPlease confirm that the data listed above is complete and current, and flag any business preferences (sites we must open or avoid, service-level expectations, or constraints not reflected here). Once confirmed, we will proceed to build the recommendation.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Warehouse network decision \u2014 which sites to open and how to serve our customers\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point on our distribution footprint. The questions are straightforward: which warehouses do we open, and how do we route product from those sites to our customers to keep costs down while meeting service expectations.\n\nWe have a defined set of candidate warehouse locations, provided in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and a corresponding set of customer locations in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Opening any warehouse entails a fixed investment and ongoing costs, summarized in {FIXED_COSTS}. Once open, shipping costs will depend on how far each facility is from each customer and the volume we move; our current transport rate is {TRANSPORT_RATE}. Each facility can only handle a limited amount of volume, as shown in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}, and every customer has a demand that needs to be fully satisfied, captured in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}.\n\nKey business rules:\n- We will only ship from sites we choose to open; unopened locations will not serve customers.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be met in full.\n- Shipping spend increases with longer hauls and higher volumes.\n\nThe objective is to minimize our total cost \u2014 the combination of facility opening costs and the shipping spend \u2014 while respecting facility capacity limits and ensuring all customer demand is covered. In practice, this means balancing the trade\u2011offs: fewer warehouses can reduce fixed costs but may increase delivery distances and risk stretching capacity; more warehouses can shorten hauls and improve coverage, but raise the number of sites we pay to open.\n\nOnce we align on this decision framework, we will produce a recommended configuration: which warehouses to open and the customer\u2011to\u2011facility allocation that delivers the lowest total cost under the above rules."
